SURVIVING THE SINGULARITY

Food: count the whole job
Begin with Chapter 9ʼs chain. Name the repetitive task, the current
method, and the result at the recipientʼs end. Count setup, supervi-
sion, maintenance, transport, and the work people still do. Donʼt turn
a machine that saves one motion into a claim that a whole job disap-
peared. Equipment needs power, spare parts, and an operatorʼs time;
count those too.

Cleanup: from litter to its destination
Hereʼs a small cleanup job, done with permission at a suitable site.
Find the litter. Collect what the local service will take. Get it into that
serviceʼs stream, not just out of the photo. Come back later and check
whether it stayed clean. Moving trash from one place to someone
elseʼs backyard isnʼt success.
A future cleanup robot might do part of that chain. To tell whether it
helps, compare the whole arrangement with an existing crew, better
bins, or a trash trap in a creek, and count the machineʼs maintenance
and recovery. “Wonʼt people just litter more?” Maybe. Maybe theyʼll
take better care of a cleaner place. Neither is a measured result, so
build in prevention and easier disposal, and look again. Trash
reaches water by the wind, runoff, and spills as well as dumping, so
the litter itself rarely tells you whoʼs to blame. EPA, Learn About
Aquatic Trash
Toxic waste is a much bigger assignment, and it belongs to properly
equipped specialists. The ambition is worth keeping: machines can
take dangerous work off peopleʼs bodies. Remotely operated robots
are already doing inspection and cleanup work in hazardous areas at
the Sellafield nuclear site in Britain. Sellafield Ltd, Spot robot deploy-
ment But identifying, containing, and disposing of dangerous mate-
rial is a job for trained people with the right equipment. Unknown
material is never a neighborhood experiment.








